<title>USA: No progress as deadline nears in US-EU meat spat.</title>
<headline>No progress as deadline nears in US-EU meat spat.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-28</dateline>
<text>
<p>The United States and European Union had informal consultations over the weekend but made no progress in resolving a meat inspection dispute that could disrupt transatlantic trade from May 1.</p>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Department spokesman Jim Petterson said there were informal conversations during the weekend but no progress was made.</p>
<p>The United States has said it would block imports of EU meat, primarily Danish pork, if there was no agreement by Wednesday in the so-called veterinary equivalency talks. Exports of U.S. poultry meat have been sidelined by the EU since April 1.</p>
<p>At issue was whether the two sides, which employ different inspection regimes, could agree the other's meat was safe.</p>
<p>An official in the U.S. meat industry said Tuesday probably would be the day when it became clear if an agreement could be reached.</p>
</text>
